{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 280,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adamantium, bolognium, dilithium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3060,
    "dDurationMs": 2779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Element Zero, Kryptonite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5839,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mythril, Netherite, Orichalcum, Unobtanium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9550,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We love the idea of fictional elements with\nmiraculous properties that science has yet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14500,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to discover."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16020,
    "dDurationMs": 5065,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is it really possible that new elements\nexist beyond the periodic table?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26650,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Science fiction in particular often imagines\nartificial, or yet-to-be-discovered elements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29970,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whose incredible properties will propel us\ninto our star-faring future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34180,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for anyone who has studied a bit of chemistry, this seems far fetched."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38660,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The elements of the periodic table are defined by the number of protons in the atomic nucleus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43930,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the atomic number - so how can there be\ngaps for new elements?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48270,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, we could keep adding protons at the\ntop end of the periodic table - but those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53180,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem to be hopelessly unstable, and so hardly useful for building warp drives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58060,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the fact is, gaps in the periodic table\ndid exist - atomic numbers that seemed to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63410,
    "dDurationMs": 2319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appear naturally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65729,
    "dDurationMs": 3551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it may also be that the current upper\nend of the periodic table is just another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such gap, beyond which may exist an island of stability containing useful elements never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before encountered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77320,
    "dDurationMs": 4643,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand the possibility of new artificial elements let's start with "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81963,
    "dDurationMs": 2857,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the story of the first artificial element."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84820,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We begin all the way back when Mendelev figured out the periodic table."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89410,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He arranged the known elements according to their atomic weight, and noticed periodic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94290,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recurrences of chemical properties as atomic weight increased."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98540,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now know that chemical properties depend on the number of outer shell or valence electrons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104320,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which increase by one every time you add a proton to the nucleus, until the shell fills"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109590,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you start over, filling the next shell\nup."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112710,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although he didn’t know about protons, Mendelev did notice gaps in his periodic table."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118280,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He correctly interpreted these as four elements that had yet to be discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123789,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He was even able to predict many of their\nproperties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126549,
    "dDurationMs": 6921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over time three of these elements were discovered and the gaps were filled with scandium, gallium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133470,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and germanium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134800,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there remained a missing element, right between Molybdenum and Ruthenium, which we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141660,
    "dDurationMs": 4667,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figured out had to correspond to a nucleus\nwith 43 protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146327,
    "dDurationMs": 6733,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For seven decades chemists searched for element 43, but it was nowhere to be found in nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153060,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was eventually discovered, but not in nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156410,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1937 Italian physicist Emilio Segrè got\nhold of some Molybdenum foil that had been"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163050,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of Ernest Lawrence’s newly invented\ncyclotron particle accelerator."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168640,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The foil has been rendered radioactive in\nthe accelerator, and Segre and his colleague"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173490,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Carlo Perrier were able to show that some\nof the Molybdenum had gained a proton, transmuting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180190,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it into element 43."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182900,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They named the new element Technetium after the Greek word for \"art\" or \"craft\", so in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187830,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sense its name means \"Crafted element\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191390,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a silvery-grey metal with chemical properties somewhere between manganese and ruthenium,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197160,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the elements above and below it on the periodic table, just as predicted by Mendelev."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202000,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why did we have to produce technetium artificially,\nwhen all the other elements can be found in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207799,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208799,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, actually technetium is produced in nature\n- just like other heavy elements, in the core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214379,
    "dDurationMs": 1871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of massive stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216250,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those elements eventually find their way into\nplanets, which form from the guts of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220440,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars after they explode as supernovae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223630,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But technetium is so unstable that by the\ntime the Earth pulled itself together from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229310,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the detritus of dead stars, all the technetium\nwas long gone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232980,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re probably familiar with the idea that\nelements can be unstable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237519,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A more common term is radioactive, which we tend to associate with very heavy elements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242540,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like uranium and plutonium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244940,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those, the instability sort of makes sense - their nuclei are enormous, so you might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250000,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect they’d have trouble holding themselves together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually any element on the periodic table can be unstable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256880,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More accurately, every element on the periodic table has unstable isotopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263130,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Isotope” is the word for different versions\nof the same element with different numbers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268570,
    "dDurationMs": 1159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269729,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a carbon atom has 6 protons in the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274410,
    "dDurationMs": 7270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The isotope of Carbon that also has 6 neutrons is called carbon-12 for its 12 total nucleons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281680,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it’s perfectly stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284560,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now an atom with 6 protons and 8 neutrons is still carbon - carbon-14, but it’s not stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290700,
    "dDurationMs": 5439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has a tendency for one of its excess neutrons to transform into a proton after ejecting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296139,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron and a neutrino, which transmutes it into nitrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301139,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The half-life of Carbon-14 is around 5700\nyears - although we do encounter it in nature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307449,
    "dDurationMs": 5101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it’s created when cosmic rays hit\nnitrogen nuclei in the atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312550,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say carbon-14 is an unstable isotope of carbon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317460,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every element has unstable isotopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320650,
    "dDurationMs": 7430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some elements only have unstable isotopes - for example, any with more than 83 protons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328080,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also weird exceptions like technetium,\nas well element 61, Promethium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334300,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are different shades of instability- for example technetium-97 has a half-life"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340889,
    "dDurationMs": 7741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 4.2 Million years, while technetium-96\nhas a half-life of 51 minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348630,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Larger atomic number tends to yield fewer\nstable isotopes and shorter half-lives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355090,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Elements with more than 118 protons decay so quickly that we’ve never been able to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359550,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect one in the lab"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361140,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, stability depends on the balance between protons and neutrons in the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367470,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might think that after a century and a\nhalf of thinking about nuclear physics, we’d"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371710,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have all of this figured out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373960,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually the dynamics of the atomic nucleus are so complicated that it takes sophisticated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380490,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computer modeling to understand any but the lightest elements - and many mysteries still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387330,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we’re getting there, so let’s see\nif we can at least lay out the competing influences"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393440,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394440,
    "dDurationMs": 6039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An atomic nucleus is a place of extreme forces in delicate balance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400479,
    "dDurationMs": 3621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand we have\nthe electromagnetic force trying to force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404100,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apart all those positively charged protons,\nand the strength of that force is great due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409830,
    "dDurationMs": 2559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the proximity of the protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412389,
    "dDurationMs": 5341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand we have the even-stronger strong nuclear force holding the nucleons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417730,
    "dDurationMs": 1279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419009,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about how the strong force holds protons and neutrons together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422780,
    "dDurationMs": 5319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The story of how it binds entire nuclei is\neven more complicated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428099,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It involves sending virtual quark packets\n- mesons - between the nucleons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434540,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The details deserve their own episode, but\nthe important thing to know is that it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439190,
    "dDurationMs": 2229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a short-range effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441419,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a nucleus gets too big, the strong force\ncan’t keep things together and various types"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446479,
    "dDurationMs": 2956,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nuclear decay become inevitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449435,
    "dDurationMs": 4315,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the strong force vanishes quickly, its strength doesn’t change much over the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453750,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "short distance where it actually works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456400,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, electromagnetism just keeps getting stronger the closer two electric charges get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462600,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means electromagnetism can overwhelm the strong force if protons are too close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467199,
    "dDurationMs": 3781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together, which is another way to destabilize the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470980,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why neutrons are so useful - they\nhelp separate protons so that the strong nuclear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476120,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force stays stronger than electromagnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479610,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For smaller nuclei - up to an atomic number of 20 - an even split of protons and neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486690,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is usually the most stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488090,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for heavier elements more and more neutrons are needed to provide that buffer, reaching"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493930,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutron-to-proton ratios of 1.5 or more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497460,
    "dDurationMs": 2549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is only part of the picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500009,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t explain why the difference of\na single neutron can mean a huge difference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505539,
    "dDurationMs": 1681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in stability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507220,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also doesn’t explain why there’s no\nstable isotope of technetium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511530,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand that we have to move beyond the common representation of the nucleus as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516190,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a muddled blob of protons and neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519110,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to think of these nucleons as having energy levels, just like electrons do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525240,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You may remember the Octet Rule from your chemistry classes: If an electron shell has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530300,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eight electrons it is stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532970,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the reason the Noble Gasses don't interact with anything, because their electron shells"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537160,
    "dDurationMs": 1970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are already complete."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539130,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something similar happens in this case, there are magic numbers which complete nuclear shells,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546050,
    "dDurationMs": 12930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126 for neutrons, and 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 114 for protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558980,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The closer a nucleus is to those numbers,\nthe more stable it will be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563290,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magic numbers are all even, and that’s\nbecause nucleons pair up according to their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570150,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum spin, just like electrons in their\nshells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574020,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One spin up and one spin down results in a net zero spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577850,
    "dDurationMs": 7940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This sort of spin coupling means that even\nif we aren’t at a magic number of protons or neutrons, nuclei"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585790,
    "dDurationMs": 8289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still prefer to have even numbers of protons, or even numbers of protons plus neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594079,
    "dDurationMs": 6421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pairs of up-down nucleons form these stable-little spin-zero partnerships in so-called nuclear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600500,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairing interactions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602640,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Having a rogue proton or neutron with an un-canceled spin seems to be bad for stability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608660,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see if we can understand the instability of Technetium using some of this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613630,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure we can see that 43 is not a magic number of protons, nor is it even like its more stable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620810,
    "dDurationMs": 2519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neighbors Molybdenum and Ruthenium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623329,
    "dDurationMs": 6471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the nearby odd numbered elements like the 47-proton’d silver have perfectly stable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629800,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isotopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631850,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even giving Technetium a magic number of 50 neutrons doesn't help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636959,
    "dDurationMs": 3041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nor does giving it a total even number of\nnucleons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640000,
    "dDurationMs": 8380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why, for example, does Technetium-97 survive for 4 million years but Tc-96 decays in under"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648380,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an hour?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649420,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems there are more mysterious forces\nat work besides neutron-padding, nuclear-shell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654560,
    "dDurationMs": 2209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filling, and spin coupling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656769,
    "dDurationMs": 4781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out there are no simple set of principles to determine nuclear stability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661550,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are so many factors at play that the\nonly way to figure this out is to simulate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667290,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668990,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ve had some remarkable success doing this using computational techniques like density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674529,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "functional theory, which we explained in a\nprevious episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678769,
    "dDurationMs": 6241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These models are still not perfect, but they\nmake many predictions we’ve verified, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685010,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some predictions that we haven’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687500,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the island of stability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691830,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we combine our experimental data with our simulations we can make graphs like this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698610,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699610,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here we can see the magic numbers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702790,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Elements with a magic number of protons have more stable isotopes, and there tend to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707950,
    "dDurationMs": 5829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more isotopes with a number of neutrons close to the neutron magic numbers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713779,
    "dDurationMs": 7021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patterns emerge, but not so as to give us\nclean rules for what is needed to yield a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720800,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stable nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722630,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As for the unstable ones like Technetium - well, they have unlucky spots in terms of not having"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728440,
    "dDurationMs": 6449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magic or even even numbers of protons, and for whatever complex reasons there is no configuration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734889,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of neutrons that can stabilize that unhappy\nnucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739190,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, are there elements not on the periodic\ntable that humans can or have invented?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745930,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, there are many."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748350,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nature can make these, but unless that production process is ongoing on the Earth, like with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754820,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Carbon-14, short-lived unstable elements are extremely rare in nature, only appearing briefly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761010,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the decay chain of other, longer-lived\nunstable elements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765790,
    "dDurationMs": 7070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first was Technetium, but we’ve now\nsynthesized 24 “artificial elements” - filling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772860,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Mendelev’s gaps, and also extending the\nperiodic table all the way 118,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779740,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oganesson, with its half-life of 0.69 milliseconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785340,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are elements beyond that, but we haven’t\nmade one last long enough to unambiguously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789980,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790980,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this isn’t the end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792480,
    "dDurationMs": 2549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ve hinted at this island of stability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795029,
    "dDurationMs": 5231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our calculations show that there may be more magic numbers for large numbers of protons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800260,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and neutrons beyond the current periodic table."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803250,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And our computer simulations agree."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805490,
    "dDurationMs": 4289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We aren’t sure what these magic numbers\nare, but apparently they are in the neighborhood"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809779,
    "dDurationMs": 6161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 184 for neutrons, and 126 for protons,\nand they could have half lives of millions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815940,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817220,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These stable-ish elements would appear here on the graph from before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823690,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A small Island of stability in an ocean of\nhopelessly unstable isotopes, but we have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830160,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not been able to reach it with the same techniques we used to craft the other artificial elements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836519,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we want to get there our conventional nuclear reactors and particle accelerators will not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841050,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be enough, we will have to come up with something new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844660,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why should we even try to get to the Island of Stability?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847950,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, it's nice to get to name an element,\nbut other than that, would such a discovery"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853589,
    "dDurationMs": 2341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have any impact on the world?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855930,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well probably, and possibly a large one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859560,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The eras of humanity are named by the materials we have mastered at the time - the Stone Age,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865290,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Bronze Age, the Iron Age, into the current Silicon Age."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870270,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And new artificial elements have proved invaluable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873230,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For instance, Technetium is used all the time in medical imaging as a contrast agent, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879740,
    "dDurationMs": 3789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in this case its short half life is actually\nan asset."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883529,
    "dDurationMs": 5221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By using an isotope with a half life of only\nsix hours we can greatly reduce the amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888750,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of radiation the patient will be exposed to,\nwhile also being able to get useful images."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894370,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plutonium is another example - no stable isotopes, but we can breed it from uranium in nuclear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899250,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reactors, where it becomes a critical part\nof the fission process in certain reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903649,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "types."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904649,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Millions of people rely on it for their electricity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908259,
    "dDurationMs": 5731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then there’s Americium, created by bombarding that plutonium in a cyclotron to kick it one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913990,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up on the periodic table before a complex\nchemical extraction process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918589,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Americium is critical for smoke detectors\n- so it’s an artificial element that has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923630,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "saved many lives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925550,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The elements we will discover in the island\nof stability will be very heavy, initially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930910,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard to synthesize, and somewhat radioactive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933850,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But some will surely have unexpected and perhaps powerful applications."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939790,
    "dDurationMs": 6969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We discover hints of these islands of possibility just beyond the bounds of the known."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946759,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What can we do but hold our breath and leap towards them, hoping to gain new ground in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952069,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humanity's journey to further and future horizons of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957860,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As always, a huge thanks to everyone who supports us on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961750,
    "dDurationMs": 2269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We seriously would not be here without you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964019,
    "dDurationMs": 5901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today’s special shoutout goes to Glenn Sugden, who’s supporting us at the quasar level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969920,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Glenn, as thanks for your generous support we’re naming element 267 after you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976490,
    "dDurationMs": 1399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sugdenium is great."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977889,
    "dDurationMs": 5741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s mildly radioactive, extremely useful\nin medical imaging, and is the critical catalyst"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983630,
    "dDurationMs": 1769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for low-temperature fusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985399,
    "dDurationMs": 3861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On top of that glows a lovely shade of violet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989260,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Glenn, thanks for your support."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990960,
    "dDurationMs": 3879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll ship you a gram of sugdenium as soon\nas its invented."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994839,
    "dDurationMs": 2901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our last episode we talked about grabby\naliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997740,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the hypothesis that says that the\nfuture emergence of new civilizations has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002449,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be cut off at some point, or else humanity is too surprisingly early to the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007819,
    "dDurationMs": 4941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the cut off is when the universe is fully colonized by … yep, grabby aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012760,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eliyah zayin has a thoughtful criticism of\nthe use of the Copernican principle to argue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018850,
    "dDurationMs": 2489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we can’t especially early in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021339,
    "dDurationMs": 6151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To paraphrase: even if there’s a very low\nchance of any given civilization being one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027490,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the first, someone has to be first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029990,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there's always going to be one civilization that ends up 'confused' about why it's so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036700,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037700,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So here you get to the most contentious aspect of anthropic reasoning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041410,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If I argue that I should find myself in the\nmost typical circumstances consistent with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046500,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "my existence, what circumstances does that preclude?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050549,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nick Bostrum’s self-sampling assumption\ntries to clear this up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055549,
    "dDurationMs": 7161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It says that all other things equal, an observer should reason as if they are randomly selected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062710,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the set of all actually existent(past, present and future) observers in their reference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068590,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "class."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069590,
    "dDurationMs": 4209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the trick is figuring out what your reference class is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073799,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if I define my reference class\nas a biological being then I don’t have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078289,
    "dDurationMs": 4351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be surprised that I don’t find myself\nsomewhere uninhabitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082640,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that permits the 4 billion years of habitability prior to my birth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088760,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why am I in the least brutal of those 4 billion years? Just lucky?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094450,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if I define my reference class as those who are able to formulate anthropic arguments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099890,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it’s less surprising that I live in\na modern civilization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105000,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then why can’t I define my reference\nclass as “those who observes an empty universe?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109980,
    "dDurationMs": 6819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, I am my mental experience, and\nthe mental experience of seeing an empty universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116799,
    "dDurationMs": 3141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is what got me asking these questions in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119940,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Being a member of an early species is one\nof the few circumstances consistent with what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125770,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I am."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126770,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could say that this is too restrictive\na way to define your reference class, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131230,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fact is there is no clearly correct way\nto do that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134660,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of this anthropic stuff is quite muddy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137470,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tristan Cleveland points out that life may\nhave had the potential to arise many times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1142890,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on early, except for the fact that once one\nincident of life got started and made progress,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149490,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would be very difficult for it to happen\na second time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153490,
    "dDurationMs": 6970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That second incidence would surely be eaten by the now-more-evolved first incidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160460,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indeed we see that the DNA off all life clearly came from a single common ancestor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166800,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if this dynamic happened on earth it makes sense it could happen across the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s exactly what the study we discussed is suggesting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176000,
    "dDurationMs": 3289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That the emergence of new civilizations is\neventually precluded."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179289,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They may not be eaten - but they can’t emerge naturally in a fully colonized universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1185250,
    "dDurationMs": 5679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several of you were pleased by the idea that we might be thought of as the ancients by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190929,
    "dDurationMs": 2361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the civilizations that followed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1193290,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example medic likes that we might one day be referred to as \"the old ones”, with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1198679,
    "dDurationMs": 3351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our ancient tec and ruins scattered across the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202030,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s just make sure we’re the all-wise,\nbenevolent type of “old ones”, rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207789,
    "dDurationMs": 2461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the tentacley Lovecraftian type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1210250,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because you know that’s another solution\nto the Fermi paradox - that the first civilization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1215980,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became cosmic horrors, obliterating anything that comes after."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220030,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like life on earth probably did."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1223690,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224770,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all this is inevitable then I guess we want\nto be early … better us than them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231280,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or we just hide?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232830,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course it’s exactly this thinking that\ngets you the dark forest solution to the Fermi"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1237860,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1238860,
    "dDurationMs": 639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dammit."
    } ]
  } ]
}
